WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.520
What happens to a synapse when the brake on its own protein making is gone?

2
00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:13.750
Fragile X syndrome is the most common inherited cause of intellectual disability.

3
00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:20.370
Its cause is a three-base repeat, CGG, near the start of the FMR1 gene.

4
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.960
Above about 200 copies, the gene is usually silenced.

5
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.500
So little or no FMRP is made.

6
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:31.820
It binds RNA and holds back translation.

7
00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:37.923
Dendrites carry their own ribosomes, so synapses can make protein within minutes.

8
00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:40.443
FMRP is a brake on that.

9
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:46.633
One signal that releases the brake comes through a glutamate receptor, mGluR5.

10
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:52.967
In hippocampal slices, activating that class of receptor makes synapses weaken for

11
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:57.127
hours. This long-term depression normally needs new protein.

12
00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.730
Predict. Take the brake away.

13
00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:02.850
What happens to that weakening?

14
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.230
And does it still need new protein?

15
00:01:11.683 --> 00:01:14.823
In fragile X mice, the weakening is exaggerated.

16
00:01:15.863 --> 00:01:18.043
Later work found it no longer needs new protein.

17
00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:21.607
Normally, the weakening waits for new protein.

18
00:01:22.487 --> 00:01:26.607
Without the brake, the usual reading goes, that protein is already there in excess.

19
00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:32.167
In 2004, Bear, Huber and Warren proposed the mGluR theory.

20
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:38.770
Without FMRP, the receptor drives too much protein synthesis, and many features

21
00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:39.770
follow.

22
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:45.673
Predict. If too much receptor signalling causes the traits, what should halving the

23
00:01:45.673 --> 00:01:46.673
receptor do?

24
00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:58.947
In 2007, Gül Dölen and Bear bred fragile X mice with one working copy of the

25
00:01:58.947 --> 00:01:59.947
receptor gene.

26
00:02:00.717 --> 00:02:05.497
Protein synthesis, spine density and sound-triggered seizures all moved toward

27
00:02:05.497 --> 00:02:06.497
typical.

28
00:02:06.967 --> 00:02:09.887
Halving the receptor shows the two genes interact.

29
00:02:10.827 --> 00:02:14.427
It may be part of the cause, or it may offset the defect another way.

30
00:02:15.417 --> 00:02:17.517
Now a different late test, with a drug.

31
00:02:18.457 --> 00:02:23.197
In adult fragile X mice, chronic treatment with a receptor blocker corrected many

32
00:02:23.197 --> 00:02:24.197
traits.

33
00:02:24.567 --> 00:02:28.247
So in mice, the circuit could still be adjusted after it was built.

34
00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:31.127
The drug does not put FMRP back.

35
00:02:32.067 --> 00:02:34.987
Drugs like this were already being tested in people.

36
00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:40.387
Trials in adolescents and adults showed no benefit over placebo on the main

37
00:02:40.387 --> 00:02:41.387
measures.

38
00:02:41.817 --> 00:02:46.037
A later trial in young children also found no benefit over placebo.

39
00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:50.647
Takeaway: a mouse rescue tests a mechanism, not a cure.
